.Dd $Mdocdate: October 27 2026 $
.Dt FUGUWEB-KEYS 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fuguweb-keys
.Nd key directory and key rotation of fuguweb
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This manual describes the key directory of a
.Xr fuguweb 1
site, and the rotation of its keys.
.Sh THE KEY DIRECTORY
An organization publishes its public keys under one prefix, so a
consumer can fetch a key and verify a release with it.
A
.Ic keys
block describes that directory, and the build publishes it.
A description can hold several such blocks, and each one names one
directory.
Two blocks must not name one directory.
.Pp
Each directory holds its own root of trust, and a binding never
crosses a directory.
A publisher that keeps two trust domains therefore keeps two
directories in one site.
The site-level files stay single: one
.Pa security.txt ,
and one Web Key Directory that gathers every address.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
keys "keys" {
	org     = fugubsd
	contact = mailto:security@fugubsd.org
	expires = 2027-09-07T00:00:00Z
	url     = https://www.fugubsd.org/keys
}

key "fugubsd-1-release" {
	status = current
	since  = 2026-09-07
}
.Ed
.Pp
The block name is the directory, one path segment below the source
directory.
The tree of a declared language takes its path in the output, so a name
of that path is refused.
Each test of the name against another name of the output folds the
two, as
.Xr fuguweb-site 7
states.
The settings are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "contact" -offset indent
.It Ic org
The organization word, the first field of every key name.
This setting is required.
Two blocks must not name one word.
A key name holds no directory name, and the serial of a purpose counts
inside one directory, so a mint of the second block would write a name
that the first block publishes.
.It Ic contact
The
.Ql Contact
field of
.Pa security.txt .
.It Ic expires
The
.Ql Expires
field of
.Pa security.txt ,
an RFC 3339 timestamp.
.Ic contact
and
.Ic expires
stand or fall together.
A site publishes one
.Pa security.txt ,
so one block alone names the two.
.It Ic url
The published prefix of the directory, an absolute URL.
.El
.Pp
Each
.Ic key
block names the stem of one key file in the directory.
The file extension gives the type:
.Pa .pub
is a
.Xr signify 1
key,
.Pa .asc
is an armored OpenPGP key, and
.Pa .pem
is one X.509 certificate.
The block sets a
.Ic status
of
.Cm current ,
.Cm next
or
.Cm retired ,
and optional
.Ic since
and
.Ic until
dates.
An OpenPGP key can set an
.Ic email
and a
.Ic fingerprint
of 40 hexadecimal characters.
A certificate can set a
.Ic fingerprint
of 64 hexadecimal characters, which is the SHA-256 of its DER form.
.Pp
One signify key of the directory is the root of trust, and its purpose
word is
.Cm root .
The current root key signs the manifest, and every other key of the
directory is a subordinate key.
A binding is a detached signature by one key over the public key file
of another key, and its name is
.Pa <target file>.<signer stem>.<ext> .
The extension is
.Pa sig
for a
.Xr signify 1
signer,
.Pa asc
for an OpenPGP signer, and
.Pa p7s
for an X.509 signer.
A binding needs no
.Ic key
block: its name holds the target, the signer and the type.
.Pp
The directory must hold a
.Pa SHA256
manifest and its
.Pa SHA256.sig
signature.
The build copies each key file, each binding file and the manifest
pair byte for byte.
It signs nothing, and it verifies no
.Pa SHA256.sig .
For the example above it generates these files:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width ".well-known/openpgpkey/hu/<hash>" -offset indent -compact
.It Pa keys/KEYS
Every OpenPGP key, when the set holds one.
.It Pa keys/index.html
The human page, with the serial, purpose, type, status, fingerprint,
dates and bindings of each key.
The row of a certificate names its subject and its validity dates
beside the fingerprint.
The page is a page of
.Ql en ,
because its own words are English.
Its title is
.Ql Keys
alone, and its head names no page in another language.
Its banner holds the site link alone, and its navigation is that of the
default language.
Each of those links steps out of the key directory into the tree of
the default language, as
.Pa ../en/index.html .
When the default language is not
.Ql en ,
each of those links carries the marks of the default language: its tag
as
.Ql lang ,
and
.Ql dir="rtl"
when that language is right to left.
The page carries no footer.
.It Pa .well-known/openpgpkey/hu/<hash>
The Web Key Directory file of each email address.
The file holds the keys of that address of every directory, in
publication order.
.It Pa .well-known/openpgpkey/policy
The Web Key Directory policy file.
.It Pa .well-known/security.txt
The RFC 9116 file, when a block sets a
.Ic contact .
The
.Ic Encryption
field names the current OpenPGP key of that block.
.El
.Pp
.Cm check
reads the source key directory, so the answer does not depend on a
build.
It checks that every key file has a block and every block has a file,
that every name matches the key name pattern or the binding name
pattern, that every purpose holds exactly one current key, that the
directory holds one current signify key of the purpose
.Cm root ,
that each binding verifies against the public key of its signer and
holds the retention rule, that the manifest names every key file and
every binding file with the digest that it has and nothing else, that
every declared fingerprint equals the computed one, and that the
signature has the shape of a
.Xr signify 1
signature.
.Pp
One report reads the whole site.
The block which names the contact and a
.Ic url
must hold a
.Cm current
OpenPGP key of its own, because the
.Ic Encryption
field names no key of another directory.
The check reports that block when another key directory holds such a
key.
A site that publishes no OpenPGP key at all writes no such field, and
that one is no problem.
.Pp
The retention rule holds each binding of the directory.
A binding whose signer is
.Cm current
or
.Cm next
must target the current root key.
A binding whose signer is
.Cm retired
must target a key of its own purpose with a higher serial.
A signify binding verifies with no command.
A binding of another type needs
.Xr gpg 1
or
.Xr openssl 1 ,
and an absent command is a problem.
.Pp
The check reads the validity of each
.Cm current
and
.Cm next
key that carries one.
It reads the expiry of an OpenPGP key with
.Xr gpg 1 ,
and the
.Ql notBefore
and
.Ql notAfter
of a certificate from the certificate itself.
An expiry that passed is a problem, and so is a certificate whose
.Ql notBefore
has not come.
An expiry within 30 days of a
.Cm current
key is a problem when its purpose holds no
.Cm next
key, because one rotation runs in two steps.
A signify key carries no date, and an OpenPGP key with no expiry
reaches no rule.
.Pp
A key verb fails for none of these reports, so
.Cm mint-key
and
.Cm import-key
each run on a directory that
.Cm check
rejects for a date.
An expired
.Cm current
key is still
.Cm current
when the verb reads its work back, so a step of that purpose could
never be made.
The 30-day report reads the whole directory, so it would fail a step
of another purpose, which writes no
.Cm next
key of the purpose that the report names.
.Pp
.Cm promote-key
fails when the key that retires is an OpenPGP key whose expiry
passed.
That key signs the chain binding, and
.Xr gpg 1
signs nothing with an expired key.
Promote the successor before the current key expires.
.Sh KEY ROTATION
Every key verb writes one key directory.
.Fl Fl dir
names it.
A description with one
.Ic keys
block needs no name, and one with several refuses a step without it.
.Pp
A consumer verifies a release against the copy of the public key that
it holds.
A release that a new key signs therefore fails in each consumer that
holds the old copy.
One rotation runs in two steps.
.Pp
One signify key of the directory is the root of trust, and its purpose
word is
.Cm root .
The current root key signs the manifest, and every other key binds to
it.
.Cm mint-key
and
.Cm import-key
refuse a key of another type for the
.Cm root
purpose, and the mint refuses before it generates one.
Every step takes the private half of the current root as
.Fl Fl signer ,
with two exceptions below.
.Pp
.Cm mint-key
generates the next key of the purpose, writes its public half into the
key directory, and writes its private half to
.Fl Fl secret
with no group and no other permissions.
The new key takes the status
.Cm next ,
or
.Cm current
when the purpose holds no current key.
.Fl Fl secret
must not name a file that exists, and must not name the signer.
A mint refuses a purpose that already holds a
.Cm next
key.
A mint of a subordinate purpose also writes the binding of the new key
over the public key file of the current root.
.Pp
.Cm promote-key
makes the
.Cm next
key of the purpose current, and retires the key that was current with
an
.Ic until
date.
.Fl Fl retiring
names the private half of the key that retires, and that key signs the
chain binding over the key which takes its place.
A consumer that trusts the old key therefore reaches the new one.
The promote removes the root binding of the retired key, and the chain
binding stays published.
A retired key keeps its file and its URL, so a release that it signed
still verifies.
.Ss The key types
.Xr signify 1
generates a
.Cm signify
key.
The public half is one comment line and one key body.
.Pp
.Xr gpg 1
generates an
.Cm openpgp
key: one Ed25519 primary key with one Curve25519 encryption subkey.
.Fl Fl email
names the address, which is the one user id of the key, and a
correspondent encrypts to the subkey.
Both halves are armored text.
The
.Ic key
block of the new key takes that
.Ic email
and the
.Ic fingerprint ,
which the command reads from the key that it generated.
.Pp
.Fl Fl expires
is a date of the form
.Ar YYYY-MM-DD ,
and the key expires at the start of that date, in UTC.
The date must be after the day of the run.
The day of the run and each earlier date fail the mint, and the reason
names the date that you gave.
A mint with no
.Fl Fl expires
makes a key with no expiry.
A key directory retires a key with an
.Ic until
date, so a rotation needs no expiry on the key itself.
.Pp
An issuer makes an
.Cm x509
certificate, such as a code signing certificate of Apple Developer ID,
so no verb mints one.
.Cm import-key
publishes it:
.Fl Fl file
names one PEM certificate, and
.Fl Fl secret
names its unencrypted PEM private key.
The verbs take no passphrase, so convert a PKCS#12 file with
.Ic openssl pkcs12
first.
The private key signs the binding of the new key, and
.Xr openssl 1
makes that detached CMS signature.
The
.Ic key
block of the new key takes the
.Ic fingerprint ,
which the command reads from the certificate that it published.
The renderer knows no issuer by name, and the root manifest vouches
for the certificate.
A renewal is a rotation: the new certificate enters as
.Cm next ,
and a promote retires the old one.
.Ss The first root mint
The first mint of the
.Cm root
purpose finds no root.
That key is current at once, it signs its own manifest, and it takes
no
.Fl Fl signer .
A directory that already holds keys takes one
.Fl Fl bind Ar stem Ns = Ns Ar path
for each current key and each next key of a subordinate purpose, and
the step writes the binding of every one of them again.
It refuses before it writes when one private half is absent.
.Pp
A directory with no
.Ic keys
block takes one from the command line:
.Fl Fl bootstrap
states the intent to make that directory,
.Fl Fl dir
names it, which defaults to
.Pa keys ,
.Fl Fl org
names the organization word, and
.Fl Fl url
names the published prefix.
A second key directory of a site arrives the same way.
.Pp
A step without
.Fl Fl bootstrap
refuses a
.Fl Fl dir
that no
.Ic keys
block names.
Each key directory holds a root of trust of its own, so a mistyped
name would otherwise publish a second root in silence.
.Cm mint-key
and
.Cm import-key
take the option, and
.Cm promote-key
makes no directory.
.Ss The root promote
A root promote signs the manifest with the key that it makes current,
so it takes
.Fl Fl secret
and no
.Fl Fl signer .
It takes one
.Fl Fl bind
for each key in force, as the first root mint does, and it removes
each binding of the retired root but the chain binding that names it.
.Pp
Each step verifies the new signature and each new binding against the
published public key of the signer before one byte reaches the key
directory, and a step that fails puts every file back.
On success the command prints one
.Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
line for each fact: the file name, the stem, the serial, the status,
the retired name of a promote, and the digest and the URL of a new key
file.
.Pp
The command reaches no network and holds no token.
The caller stores the private key and commits the public one.
